Talking about benchmarking exact.matches() (in another thread), it is
interesting to see that exact.matches()[[1]] is twice as fast as
regmatches(). I would have thought that regmatches(), being included in
the base package now, would be faster than exact.matches()[[1]], but
that doesn't seem to be the case. Take a look:

> aa<-c("some sentence here, whatever it may be", "and another one here too")
> aa<-rep(aa, 50000)
> search.term<-"here"
>
> system.time({
+     bb<-gregexpr(search.term, aa, perl=T)
+     cc<-unlist(regmatches(aa, bb))
+ })
   user  system elapsed
  1.509   0.109   1.613
>
> source("http://www.linguistics.ucsb.edu/faculty/stgries/exact_matches.r")
> system.time({
+     dd<-exact.matches(search.term, aa)[[1]]
+ })
   user  system elapsed
  0.786   0.071   0.851
>
> identical(cc, dd)
[1] TRUE

